;; Docs for std/stopwatch.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "stopwatch.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Stopwatches that read the ambient clock, and time-it to measure a body.")
  (see Stopwatch stopwatch time-it)
  (example """
    (import (std stopwatch))

    (def sw (stopwatch))
    (stopwatch-start! sw)
    (do-the-work)
    (stopwatch-stop! sw)
    (stopwatch-ms sw)          ; elapsed milliseconds, as a long
    (ref sw 's)                ; the same, in whole seconds

    (time-it #:times 10 (do-the-work))
    """)
  (reference """
    A stopwatch reads the prelude's @code{monotonic-ms}, which reads
    @code{current-clock}. It does not hold a
    @code{System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch}. Thus a test can bind a clock and get
    an exact elapsed time instead of a real one:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (parameterize ((current-clock (make-clock #:monotonic-ms (fun () 0L))))
      (time-the-thing))   ; every stopwatch inside reads 0
    }|

    A stopwatch is mutable. It is started and stopped, and the time of each
    run is added to its total. The resolution is one millisecond.

    A stopwatch is @code{Refable} by unit: @code{(ref sw 'ms)} is the elapsed
    time in milliseconds and @code{(ref sw 's)} is the elapsed time in whole
    seconds, rounded down. Any other symbol raises
    @code{System.ArgumentException}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Stopwatches
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Stopwatch
  (summary "A stopwatch: a total elapsed time, and the reading of the run in progress.")
  (field started "The clock reading at the last start, or -1 when the watch is not running.")
  (field elapsed "The milliseconds that the completed runs add up to.")
  (see stopwatch stopwatch-start! stopwatch-ms)
  (reference """
    Both fields are mutable, because a stopwatch is started and stopped in
    place. Use the functions of this module to change them, not
    @code{record-set!}.

    @code{(ref sw 'ms)} and @code{(ref sw 's)} read the elapsed time, in
    milliseconds or in whole seconds. Any other symbol raises
    @code{System.ArgumentException}.
    """))

(:doc stopwatch
  (summary "A new stopwatch: stopped, at zero.")
  (returns "A stopwatch that is not running and has 0 ms elapsed.")
  (see stopwatch-start!))

(:doc stopwatch-start!
  (summary "Starts a stopwatch.")
  (arg sw "The stopwatch.")
  (see stopwatch-stop! stopwatch-ms)
  (reference """
    Starting a watch that is running does nothing. This is the same as
    @code{Start} on a .NET stopwatch.
    """))

(:doc stopwatch-stop!
  (summary "Stops a stopwatch and adds the run to its total.")
  (arg sw "The stopwatch. Stopping a watch that is not running does nothing.")
  (see stopwatch-start! stopwatch-ms))

(:doc stopwatch-ms
  (summary "The elapsed time of a stopwatch, in milliseconds.")
  (arg sw "The stopwatch.")
  (returns "The total of the completed runs, plus the run in progress if the watch is running.")
  (see stopwatch-stop!)
  (reference """
    You can read a running watch. It does not stop.
    """))

(:doc get-memory-usage
  (summary "The bytes allocated on the calling thread since it started.")
  (returns "A long that only increases. Subtract two readings to get what was allocated between them.")
  (see time-it)
  (reference """
    @code{System.GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread}. It counts the calling
    thread only, so a reading does not include allocations of other threads
    or other fibers.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; time-it
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc time-it
  (summary "Runs a body, then prints the mean time and memory allocated per run.")
  (form (time-it body ...))
  (form (time-it #:times n body ...))
  (arg n "How many timed runs. One when it is not given.")
  (rest body "What to time. At least one form. Its value is dropped.")
  (see stopwatch get-memory-usage)
  (example """
    (time-it #:times 100 (list-sort big-list))
    ;; prints: Mean of 100 runs: 3ms. 120000B allocated per run.
    """)
  (reference """
    The body runs once as a warmup before the timed runs, so a body with
    @code{#:times n} runs n + 1 times. During the warmup,
    @code{current-output-port} is a string port, and its output is
    discarded. Other side effects of the warmup occur.

    The time comes from a stopwatch, so it reads @code{current-clock}, and
    it is in whole milliseconds. The mean is an integer division, so a body
    that takes less than a millisecond can show 0ms. The memory comes from
    @code{get-memory-usage}, so it counts only the calling thread. A body
    that suspends and continues on a different thread gives an incorrect
    memory value.

    The form returns unit.
    """))
